  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 402 AM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nWhile the overall forecast scenario for the long term hasn't changes \nsignificantly, the only caveat will be the arrival of the remnants \nof Dolly in the form of a broad area of low pressure and enhanced \ndeep tropical moisture. NBM and long-range guidance has responded by \npushing max POPs into the 80-90% range across much of SoFlo each day \nthrough the weekend. Dew points, PWATs and rainfall estimates all\nremain above or well above seasonal normals through the long \nterm, supporting the forecast philosophy of having a very active, \nwet weather pattern. \n\nMeanwhile, the W Atl sfc ridge will slowly push southward and closer \nto the area. This will shift overall flow more southerly, \nenhancing even more the moisture advection. In general, expect \nscattered to numerous showers and storms each afternoon/early \nevening. Best chances for strong convection will again happen with\nthe onset of the East coast sea breeze, first along the Atlantic \nmetro areas early in the afternoon, then pushing inland during the\nlate afternoon hours. Hazards will include gusty winds, heavy \nrainfall and frequent lightning.\n\nTemperatures are expected to remain seasonably hot each day, with \nafternoon highs in the low to mid 90s. Overnight lows should remain \nin the low 80s across the coastlines, and mid 70s over the\ninterior.",
